# u014 — `record -w`: recording one window of `wm` — done

**Type:** tools · **Owner:** tools track · **Priority:** P2 · **Status:** done (2026-10-06) · **Blocked by:** — (093 done) · **Roadmap:** track G · **Constitution:** MC-3.3, MC-10.2

## Problem

`record` (issue 093) records the whole screen in front. A demo or a bug report of one program in `wm` would rather have just its window, at the window's size, whatever else is on the screen.

The window's pixels are in its surface, the window broker's memory (157). The program that drew it and `wm` hold leases of it; the shell does not. Text windows hold cells, which need to be drawn (the 8×16 font) to become pixels.

## Plan

- `wm` starts `record` for a window: from its run line (`record -w`, the window in front) or from a key of its own.
  - `wm` lends `record` a read-only lease of that window's surface, minted from its own, and nothing else of the window.
  - No program records another's window unless the user starts it.
- `record` draws the frames itself:
  - a pixel window's content as it is;
  - a text window's cells with `mind::font16`, as `wm` draws them.
  
  The frame has the surface's size when the recording starts. (Decided: a window resized while it records keeps that size in the file, its content cut at the right and bottom or black around it; scaling would blur a text window's cells.)
- The compositor's dot shows the recording as for the screen. `record` captures through the surface, not the compositor, so `wm` draws a red dot in the window's title while it lends a lease to a recorder.

## Acceptance criteria

QEMU `wm` suite: `record -w -t 2 ram:win.avi` typed in the run line with the clock's window in front. The recording has 20 frames of the window's content size (320 × 176), more than one of them coded (the clock's seconds), and the mark is on the clock window's frame while it records, gone after. Host tests: a text surface drawn as a frame holds its cells in the 8×16 font in their colours (the QEMU `wm` suite's disk cannot be read back, so the decoded-frame check of a text window moved to the host); the mark on the frame.

## Related

[093](093-screen-recording.done), [088](088-text-window-manager.done), [157](157-window-broker.done), [165](165-display-client-for-programs.done).

## Resolution

- **`wm`** (`wm/src/main.rs`): a program started from the run line (or the menu) that asks for `REQUEST_DISPLAY` gets, in `SLOT_DISPLAY`, a read-only lease of the window in front: `lease` mints `CAP_READ | CAP_GRANT` from the surface the broker gives `wm`. Nothing else of the window, and not the screen (`wm` holds no compositor client). A console program runs in a `console` started for it, and the lease goes to that console. The log says `(WINDOW id TO SEE)`.
- **Loader** (`idl/loader.wit` 1.3): `grant-memory` lends a memory capability. It takes `SLOT_DISPLAY` only, and refuses a writable capability (`rights`). `grant` stays for endpoints: its wire check refused the lease.
- **The mark** (`wm/src/desk.rs`): `Win::recording` draws " ● REC " (white on red) at the left of the window's frame. It stays for the recording's `-t` and 3 s more, or until the program `wm` started ends. `[WM] RECORDING id` and `[WM] RECORDED id` are logged.
- **`console`**: passes the memory capability it holds in `SLOT_DISPLAY` (`grant-memory`) to the first program that asks for `REQUEST_DISPLAY`, then drops it.
- **`record`**:
  - With a memory capability in `SLOT_DISPLAY` it records that window. It maps the lease and keeps the capability, which the broker revokes when the window ends.
  - Before each frame it checks that the capability is still there and that the window is not being closed.
  - A frame whose program has not drawn since the last (the surface's change counter) repeats the last one.
  - `-w` without a window says to start it from `wm`'s run line.
  - When `wm` leaves (Alt+Q), the broker revokes its leases and the recording stops ("the window closed").
  - Left open: a lease revoked in the millisecond `record` copies the window ends `record` with a fault. The revocation unmaps the window; the file keeps the header written within the last second. This happens when `wm` leaves, or when the program ends by itself (the broker revokes once `wm` has listed the windows without it, up to 250 ms later).
- **`mind::window::Surface::draw`**: the content as a frame of pixels. A pixel surface as it is; a text surface's cells in the 8×16 font, as `wm` draws them.
- Tests:
  - QEMU `wm` suite (Alt+R, `record -w -t 2 ram:win.avi` for the clock; the summary read from the console's window; the mark on and off);
  - `tests/window_host.rs` (`a_surface_drawn_as_a_frame`);
  - `tests/wm_host.rs` (`a_recorded_window_is_marked`).
- Docs: README (`record`), docs/tools §4.9 and its Russian copy, docs/api (slot 15, `grant-memory`).
